This is a common issue with .NET MAUI on .NET 10. Android deprecated `AdjustResize` (API 30+), and .NET 10 MAUI adopts **edge-to-edge rendering** by default, which changes keyboard handling entirely.

## The Fix: Use `Ime` Window Insets

Instead of the old soft input mode, you now react to **keyboard insets**. The simplest approach:

### Option 1 — Let MAUI handle it automatically

MAUI 10 includes `KeyboardAutoManagerScroll`. Make sure you haven't disabled it:

```csharp
// In MauiProgram.cs — do NOT add this line (it disables auto-scroll):
// .ConfigureMauiHandlers(h => KeyboardAutoManagerScroll.Disconnect());
```

If your layout uses a `ScrollView` or is inside a page that supports scrolling, this should just work.

### Option 2 — Pad the bottom with insets (recommended for chat UIs)

Add bottom padding that reacts to the keyboard in your Android platform code:

```csharp
// Platforms/Android/MainActivity.cs
using Android.Views;
using AndroidX.Core.View;
using Microsoft.Maui.Controls.Compatibility.Platform.Android;

public class MainActivity : MauiAppCompatActivity
{
    protected override void OnCreate(Bundle? savedInstanceState)
    {
        base.OnCreate(savedInstanceState);

        ViewCompat.SetOnApplyWindowInsetsListener(
            Window!.DecorView.FindViewById(Android.Resource.Id.Content)!,
            new KeyboardInsetsListener());
    }

    private class KeyboardInsetsListener : Java.Lang.Object, IOnApplyWindowInsetsListener
    {
        public WindowInsetsCompat OnApplyWindowInsets(View view, WindowInsetsCompat insets)
        {
            var imeInsets = insets.GetInsets(WindowInsetsCompat.Type.Ime());
            var navInsets = insets.GetInsets(WindowInsetsCompat.Type.NavigationBars());

            // Use the larger of keyboard or nav-bar bottom inset
            int bottom = Math.Max(imeInsets.Bottom, navInsets.Bottom);
            view.SetPadding(0, 0, 0, bottom);

            return insets;
        }
    }
}
```

### Option 3 — Quick XAML-only workaround

If you just need it working fast, wrap your chat layout so the input bar sits above the keyboard:

```xml
<Grid RowDefinitions="*, Auto">
    <CollectionView Grid.Row="0" ItemsSource="{Binding Messages}" />
    <Entry Grid.Row="1" Placeholder="Type a message..."
           Margin="{OnPlatform Android='0,0,0,0'}" />
</Grid>
```

Combined with Option 1 (the default auto-manager), this layout should adjust automatically.

---

**TL;DR:** Remove any `AdjustResize` calls — they're no-ops now. For a chat screen, **Option 2** (insets listener) gives you the most reliable, flicker-free result. The keyboard's `Ime` inset tells you exactly how much to pad.
